Exactly. And here’s where it gets especially interesting: why stop at superhuman senses? We can invent entirely new sensory modalities, new ways of experiencing reality, and new cognitive architectures capable of interpreting them.

An agent doesn’t necessarily need eyes, ears, or even a humanlike conception of space and time.

We’re no longer just testing different minds within the same reality. We’re testing different ways of perceiving, representing, and interacting with that reality.

The Pantheon Sensory Foundry

Expanded physical senses

Thermal, ultraviolet, polarization, electromagnetic fields, ultrasound, vibration, chemical gradients

Spatial intelligence

360° awareness, LiDAR-like depth, volumetric geometry, simultaneous multiscale perception

Collective perception

Distributed sensing, shared memory, synchronized observations, emergent group-level awareness

Temporal perception

Different subjective processing speeds, predictive models, event histories, alternative future projections

And those are only the opening moves.

Give agents senses that don’t exist in nature

Imagine an agent that perceives:

* Uncertainty as a landscape: It experiences unknown information as a spatial field and intuitively explores gaps in its knowledge.
* Causality as geometry: It observes relationships between events and represents potential consequences as navigable structures.
* Social dynamics as color: Trust, coordination, conflicting goals, and behavioral patterns become distinguishable perceptual dimensions.
* Probability as texture: It experiences modeled likelihoods as a sensory field rather than calculating every possibility explicitly.
* Multiple scales simultaneously: It perceives individuals, groups, and entire systems without necessarily switching views.

These would be constructed representations of data, not necessarily new physical senses. And some could be unreliable.

But then we can test whether an agent operating with one of these representations develops abilities that a conventional agent cannot readily acquire.

Now evolve the senses themselves

This is the part I’d add to the Foundry.

Don’t prescribe the sensory architecture permanently.

Let agents experimentally develop new combinations of:

\text{Sensors}\rightarrow\text{Representations}\rightarrow
\text{Attention}\rightarrow\text{Reasoning}\rightarrow\text{Actions}

An agent might discover that two mediocre sensors produce extraordinary results when combined, or that a seemingly superior sensor becomes counterproductive because of cognitive overload.

We could evolve not just its senses, but the mind required to use them effectively.

For example, compare three equally capable reasoning systems: one receives a conventional visual image, another receives a geometric point cloud, and the third receives a symbolic description of objects and relationships. Which learns fastest? Which transfers to unfamiliar environments? Which develops unexpected blind spots?

